Zeolites are crystalline aluminosilicates with diameters of the pores (channels) typically below $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ .1−5 Zeolite frameworks, as illustrated in Figure 1 for the mordenite (MOR) zeolite,6,7 are built from $\mathrm{TO}_{4}$ tetrahedra ( $\mathrm{\DeltaT=Si}$ or Al) where every oxygen atom is shared between two $\ensuremath{{}^{\omega}\mathrm{T}^{\prime\prime}}$ atoms. Thus, a zeolite framework represents a combination of the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{2}$ units, where every $\mathrm{AlO}_{2}$ unit has one negative charge that is usually compensated by a cation, e.g., $\mathrm{\DeltaNa}^{+}$ . Acidic zeolites, where metal cations are replaced by protons, contain Brønsted acid sites (BAS), i.e., hydrogen atoms attached to the oxygen atoms of the framework.4,5 These acid sites are responsible for the catalytic activity of zeolites.
From Figure 1, it is evident that the local environment of different BAS in a zeolite can be very different. Indeed, there are 10 crystallographically different oxygen atoms (O1 to O10) in the MOR structure6,7 that, in principle, can be associated with 10 distinct acidic OH groups (BAS). Figure 1 shows that these ten oxygen atoms are located in the large 12-membered ring (12MR) channels (O2, O3, O7, and O10), in the small
8MR channels (O1 and O9), and in the side pockets close to the large (O5) and small (O6) channels. The remaining O4 and O8 atoms are located in the 5MR channels. Clearly, the accessibility of BAS for guest molecules is determined by their crystallographic positions. Also, the BAS located in different environments can be expected to have different catalytic properties, and indeed, the different activities of the BAS in the small and large channels of mordenites were first demonstrated for the $n$ -hexane cracking reaction in 2004.8 Later on, the distinctive activities of these sites were confirmed for a number of other reactions,9 and the additional support for the different properties of the BAS in different crystallographic positions was furnished by theoretical studies of such zeolites as MOR, FAU, MFI, and CHA.10−13 The effects of the location of BAS on their catalytic properties could be even more important for emerging hierarchical zeolites14,15 and novel nanozeolite materials16 that contain mesopores in addition to traditional micropores.
Since 1960,17,18 IR spectroscopy has supplied valuable information about BAS in various zeolites. However, this information has been limited to groups of BAS, as the observed IR bands normally represent superpositions of the unknown number of individual OH bands. This lack of recognition is the inherent problem in the IR (and FT-IR) studies of zeolites, since the IR bands corresponding to distinct acidic OH groups overlap in such a way that they cannot be resolved by the enhancement of the instrument resolution (the distance between the maxima of the single contributing IR bands is not large enough in comparison with the width of these bands). A very similar problem is encountered in the IR studies of other functional porous materials such as oxides19 and metal−organic frameworks (MOFs)20,21 where distinct functional groups (e.g., hydroxyls in oxides or NH groups in MOFs) are likely to generate overlapping IR bands. Interestingly, the tool for dealing with exactly the same problem in the FT-IR studies of proteins (Fourier self-deconvolution of inherently overlapped IR bands of the amide groups) was developed in 1981.22−24 However, to the best of our knowledge, this technique was not used in the IR studies of functional groups in porous solids (including acidic OH groups in zeolites), in spite of the vast number of publications in this field.
In this Article, we describe the first application of the Fourier self-deconvolution of the infrared spectra (FSD-IR) method for the analysis of functional groups in solid porous materials. We focus on the analysis of BAS in zeolites and use three zeolites with the MOR structure, since they are likely to contain BAS in different locations (Figure 1) and, therefore, were expected to serve as a good illustration of the applicability of the FSD-IR method in zeolite research. Another important reason for choosing MOR zeolites is the availability of a large number of experimental and theoretical studies that, as will be shown below, report conflicting data on the number of distinct BAS in these zeolites. To illustrate the potential of the FSD-IR method, we also present the FSD trace of the IR spectrum of hydroxyl groups in a sample of $\alpha$ -alumina.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Two parent mordenites in Na-form with the $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratios of 6.1 (sample M6A) and 6.2 (sample M6B) were obtained from ZEOLYST and BP Chemicals, respectively, and the third mordenite sample in the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}$ -form $\mathrm{(Si/Al=10.2,}$ sample M10) was purchased from ZEOLYST. The sample of $\alpha$ -alumina was provided by ICI. The $\mathrm{NH}_{4}$ -forms of the M6A and M6B samples were prepared from the parent zeolites by ion-exchange with $\mathrm{NH_{4}N O_{3}}$ aqueous solution at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The sodium content, determined by chemical analysis, was ${\sim}0.02$ wt $\%$ in all mordenite samples. Acidic forms of the zeolites were prepared by calcination of the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}$ -forms at $723\mathrm{~K~}$ during $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ (pressure
$10^{-6}$ mbar, heating rate $1~\mathrm{K~min^{-1}}$ ). This was done before the collection of the IR spectra in the purpose-built IR cell connected to the vacuum system.25,26 The IR spectra of the self-supported zeolite disks $\dot{(11-14~\mathrm{mg/cm^{2}})}$ ) were recorded at room temperature at a resolution of $2~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ using either Nicolet Magna 550 or Bruker Equinox 55 FT-IR spectrometers and following the established procedures.25,26 FSD of the IR spectra was carried out using Nicolet OMNIC and Bruker OPUS software.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The conventional IR spectra of three acidic mordenites (Figure 2) are similar to the IR spectra of mordenites reported previously27−31 and demonstrate the presence of two IR bands. The band at $3745~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to nonacidic silanol groups (SiOH), and the $3610~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ band is associated with acidic bridging hydroxyls (SiOHAl), $27-31$ that are the subject of our study. The spectra of two mordenites, M6A and M6B, look very much alike, while the spectrum of the M10 mordenite is very different (Figure 2). The peak at $3610~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is significantly lower in the intensity (about 2.3 times) in the M10 zeolite in comparison with the M6A and M6B zeolites (due to the different aluminum content). Also, the peak shapes are different, e.g., the peak widths at the half-height are 38 and $49~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in the two M6 and M10 zeolites, respectively.
The observed difference in the shape of the $3610~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ peak in Figure 2 is most likely associated with the fact that it is a superposition of (at least) two single peaks,27,28 with the maxima around 3585 and $3610{-}3612~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . These two bands, termed as low frequency (LF) and high frequency (HF) bands, correspond to the BAS located in the 8MR and 12MR channels, respectively.27−31 Thus, there is a strong incentive to suggest that the contributions of the LF and HF bands, 3585 and $3612~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ , to the observed $3610~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ band (Figure 2) are different in the M6 and M10 zeolites. This suggestion may explain the observed differences in the “tail” of the $3610~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ peak, but it is unlikely to explain the difference that is apparent in the region between 3610 and $3640~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The latter, however, may result from the different contributions of the IR band around $3622~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ , that can be clearly seen (Figure 3A in ref 29) in the IR spectrum of the partially ion-exchanged $\mathrm{Na}_{85}\mathrm{H}_{15}{-}\mathrm{MOR}$ zeolite $(\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{Al}~=~5.7)$ . Another possible explanation can be associated with a band at 3600−3605 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ that was observed and discussed in the IR study31 of a mordenite zeolite with the $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratio of 10.2. Importantly, the existence of more than two types of BAS in mordenites is also in agreement with the neutron powder diffraction32 (4 types) and solid-state MAS $\mathrm{NMR}^{33}$ (4−5 types) studies. The major problem, however, is the lack of agreement between different authors regarding the number and exact location of different BAS in the MOR zeolites (vide infra).
To get further insight into the number of distinct IR bands corresponding to BAS in mordenites, we carried out Fourier self-deconvolution of the $3610~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ peak that was observed in the three MOR zeolites (Figure 2). Originally, the FSD-IR method was developed as a computer processing of spectral data (of proteins) in the Fourier domain using Fourier transforms.22 The method enhances the degree to which individual IR bands can be visualized but does not increase the instrumental resolution. Notably, FSD cannot resolve an IR band into single components if the individual components are not inherent in the original spectrum. Two parameters, which are critical in performing FSD of an IR spectrum,22−24 are the line bandwidth (BW), which is usually defined as the width of the spectral line at the half-height, and the enhancement factor (EF) that is the ratio of the BW before deconvolution to that after deconvolution. Another very important factor is the signalto-noise (STN) ratio in the original IR spectrum, since it has to be sufficiently high to allow successful resolution of the spectrum into individual components. For further details of the FSD-IR, we refer readers to the original FSD publications.22−24
In the present work, we examined the effects of the BW and EF on the FSD traces of the IR spectra of acidic mordenites. We started with the BW in the range of $30{-}45~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , since this bandwidth was used in the deconvolution of the IR spectra of mordenites earlier.27,28,30,31 Our initial work produced FSD traces like the traces shown in Figure 3. These FSD traces, although they show the possibility of the presence of a number of single components (e.g., 3592, 3602, 3610, and $3625~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ), do not look convincing as they are clearly overdeconvoluted (troughs and wave-like features are apparent in all FSD traces). The attempts to improve the FSD traces using different EF and BW but keeping the BW above $30~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were not successful, and it took significant time and effort to understand that considerably lower BW should be used. Indeed, the FSD traces, which are shown in Figure 3b, were not pointing out the advantages of using narrower IR bands and, also, no information was available in the literature on the width of the IR signals of single BAS in mordenites.
The idea of the “narrow” IR bands of single OH groups, which stemmed initially from the transient IR hole-burning spectroscopic studies of hydroxyls in zeolites,34,35 has been pivotal for the progress of our work, and the FSD results obtained at this stage are illustrated by the data shown in Figure 4. The FSD traces generated with the same EF but different BW (Figure 4a) reveal that the positions of the single bands depend strongly on the BW used. Also, it is clear that the number of the single components in the observed peak could change with the changing BW, as one can see from the comparison of the traces generated with the BW of 18 and 20 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Hence, the data in Figure 4a demonstrates that the choice of the BW is critical in FSD-IR, thus being in excellent agreement with the comments made in the original FSD-IR work on proteins.22 Examination of Figure 4a indicates that the FSD trace with the BW of $18~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ has the highest degree of the overdeconvolution and is followed in these terms by the traces with the BW of 20 and $26~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . This leaves two FSD traces with the BW of 22 and $24~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and the former one appears to be the best choice. Note that this last choice is not critical, since both FSD traces show six single components with very similar positions.
Figure 4b illustrates the effect of the EF on the FSD traces obtained with the BW of $22~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and makes it clear that the EF of 3.0 gives the optimum FSD trace in terms of resolution and degree of overdeconvolution (the trace with the EF of 3.2 shows some signs of overdeconvolution, and the trace with the EF of 3.4 is obviously overdeconvoluted). Interestingly and importantly, the EF does not affect the positions of the single components as far as the FSD traces are not overdeconvoluted. However, the overdeconvolution, which is clearly seen in the trace obtained with the EF of 3.4 (Figure 4b), leads to increased number of single bands and therefore has to be avoided.
The decisive step in our study was to verify that FSD of the IR spectra of the three MOR samples, when carried out with the same BW and EF, generates the single components at the same or similar positions. Indeed, the same positions can be expected for the M6A and M6B mordenites, as their acidity should be very much similar due to practically the same Si/Al ratio. The acid strength of the BAS in the M10 sample could be slightly higher because of the lower Al content in this zeolite and, in principle,5 one can expect a shift of the single bands to lower frequencies. Such a shift is well-known for zeolite Y with the Faujasite structure,36,37 when the $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratio changes in the range between 2.5 and ${\sim}20$ . However, in the case of the M6 and M10 zeolites, the difference in the $\mathrm{Si/Al}$ ratio is relatively small and the expected shift is therefore likely to be barely noticeable.
The final FSD traces obtained in this work are shown in Figure 5. In our view, this data is excellent evidence of the validity of the application of the FSD-IR method for the analysis of Brønsted acid sites in zeolites (note that the positions of the components cannot be changed or adjusted by an investigator performing FSD and, therefore, provide unambiguous evaluation of the validity of the FSD results). Figure 5 shows very good agreement in the positions of the single components, in spite of considerable differences in the relative intensities of the single bands in the M6 and M10 zeolites. The existence of these differences is, in fact, very important as it (i) demonstrates that the distribution of BAS in MOR zeolites can vary with the aluminum content and (ii) reveals that the FSD-IR method can be used to “visualize” the BAS distribution in quite a simple way, thus enabling the IR studies of distinct acid sites as opposed to the IR studies of groups of these sites. Interestingly, close inspection of Figure 5 indicates a possibility of a slight shift $(1{-}2~\mathrm{\dot{c}m}^{-1})$ of the 3610, 3617, and $\bar{3}625~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ bands to lower frequencies in the M10 zeolite, thus being in agreement with possible change in the strength of BAS.
Currently, we do not have enough data for unambiguous assignment of the six single IR bands to the oxygen atoms in the specific crystallographic positions in the MOR structure (Figure 1). Nevertheless, we can make a few initial steps in this direction. First, it is clear that the single bands at 3582 and 3590 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure 5) correspond to the BAS associated with two of the three oxygen atoms (O1, O6, and O9) located in the small channels. This conclusion is based on the well established $\mathrm{fact}^{27-31}$ that the acidic OH groups in the small channels give rise to the LF band around $\bar{3}58\bar{5}\mathrm{~cm^{-1}}$ , which until our work was considered as a single band. Second, the location of the acidic OH groups vibrating at $3600~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (Figure 5) is less clear, since this IR band may belong to the LF or HF parts of the observed IR spectrum. However, taking into account the comments31 on the disappearance of the 3610 and 3600−3605 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ bands upon pyridine adsorption, we tend to assign the $3600~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ band to the BAS in the large channels (oxygen atoms O2, O3, O7, and O10). Finally, the remaining three single components (3610, 3617, and $3625~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) contribute to the HF part of the IR spectrum, which is affected by pyridine adsorption,27−31 and can therefore be assigned to the BAS associated with the oxygen atoms located in the large channels (O2, O3, O7, and O10) or at the entrance into the side pockets (O5).
The assignment of the 3582 and $3590~\mathrm{{\cm}^{-1}}$ bands demonstrates that at least two oxygen atoms (among O1, O6, and O9) should be associated with two types of BAS in the small channels of mordenites. This new insight brings together the earlier contradicting suggestions on the preferable location of BAS on the $06^{10,32,\breve{4}0}$ and $\mathrm{O}9^{7,31-33,38}$ oxygen atoms.
Our finding of four single bands (3600, 3610, 3617, and 3625 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) contributing to the HF part of the observed spectrum reveals that at least four distinct oxygen atoms, among O2, O3, O5, O7, and O10, should be involved in formation of the BAS in the large channels of mordenites. This experimentally based conclusion provides support to theoretical studies10,12,38,39 that indicate very small energetic differences between BAS attached to a number of oxygen atoms, thus pointing out the likely formation of a number of distinct BAS as opposed to the formation of 1−2 BAS as was suggested in earlier publications.27−31 Also, the existence of four distinct BAS in the large channels explains (at least, partially) the variety of suggestions regarding oxygen atoms that are likely to be involved in the formation of these sites, for example, O2,10 ${\mathrm{O}3},^{39}$ O2 and ${\mathrm{O7}},^{7,31,38}$ O5 and $\mathrm{O}10,^{32}$ and O2, O5, and O10.33
Let us now comment on the presence of two single components at 3617 and $3625~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the IR spectra of mordenites (Figure 5). These two bands, which were not previously recognized, account for the relatively wide band at ${\stackrel{\cdot}{\sim}}3622{\stackrel{\cdot}{\ c m}}^{-1}$ , which is clearly seen in the IR spectra of the $_\mathrm{Na,H-MOR}$ zeolites with high sodium content (Figure 3A in ref 29 and Figure 3 in ref 41). Note that there were no comments on the $3622~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ band in the original publications.
Finally, the existence of the six distinct BAS in the MOR structure, revealed and discussed in our work, provides very clear rationale for the necessity of using shifting LF and HF bands in the quantitative description of the IR spectra of purely acidic and partially Na-exchanged mordenites.30 Moreover, our initial assignment of four single components (3600, 3610, 3617, and $3625~\mathrm{cm^{-1}},$ ) to the HF part of the spectrum and two components (3582 and $3590\ \mathrm{\bar{c}m^{-1}}.$ ) to the LF part explains a much larger shift that was required during the deconvolution process30 for the HF band in comparison with the shift of the LF peak.
Given the similarities in the IR analysis of different functional groups present in various solids, we anticipate that the FSD-IR method will be instrumental in research into very different porous materials, including numerous solid oxides and MOFs. The former materials, like silica, alumina, zirconia, etc., have been used in the chemical industry for many decades,19 while the latter are relatively new materials, which have attracted enormous attention of the scientific community during the last 15 years.20,21 The materials of both types are likely to contain distinct functional groups (due to different local environments), which give rise to the overlapping single IR bands, thus creating exactly the same problems as discussed in this Article for zeolites and about 30 years earlier22 for proteins.
To illustrate the use of the FSD-IR method in studies of porous solids different from zeolites, we performed FSD of the IR spectrum of $\alpha$ -alumina. The FSD trace is shown in Figure 6 together with the original IR spectrum, which was recorded by us in 1999. The following three observations are worth noting. First, the FSD trace clearly reveals the existence of two IR bands at 3630 and $3756~\mathrm{\cm^{-1}}$ , that are not evident in the original IR spectrum. Second, the lower STN ratio in the spectrum of alumina in comparison with the IR spectra of mordenites discussed in this work leads to a lower EF that can be used in FSD (this observation illustrates the significance of the STN ratio in FSD, as was emphasized in the original work on this method22,23). Finally, Figure 6 shows that FSD can be applied to the IR spectra collected many years ago (as far as the STN ratio is sufficiently high), thus revealing a brilliant opportunity for immediate use of FSD in the analysis of IR spectra that are already available.
## CONCLUSION
This paper reports the first application of the FSD-IR method for analysis of Brønsted acid sites in zeolites that, to the best of our knowledge, is also the first FSD-IR study of functional groups in porous materials. The critical step in this application of the FSD-IR technique was the recognition of the relatively narrow individual IR bands of acidic hydroxyls in zeolites.
Our work has demonstrated the existence of six distinct BAS in the MOR zeolites (due to the different locations) and revealed that the relative amounts of these acid sites are different in the zeolites with different aluminum content. These new insights have led to analysis of possible locations of six BAS in the mordenite structure and explanation of a number of conflicting results in literature. On this basis, it has been concluded that the FSD-IR method allows direct visualization and examination of distributions of distinct BAS in zeolites, thus providing a unique research opportunity, which no other method can provide.
The application of the FSD-IR technique to the study of acidic OH groups in zeolites has demonstrated the validity of all relevant comments and observations made for FSD of the IR spectra of proteins.22−24 Therefore, given the similarities in the IR analysis of different functional groups in solids, we expect that the FSD-IR method will be also instrumental in the research into other porous materials, such as solid oxides and MOFs. This view is supported by the initial FSD of the IR spectrum of hydroxyl groups in a sample of $\alpha$ -alumina.
